  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nThis afternoon...The inland areas, mountains and deserts are a \nfew degrees warmer than at this time yesterday while the coastal \nareas are a few degrees cooler as onshore flow and a sea breeze\nbegin to spread marine air inland. Skies are clear except for a\nfew low clouds that formed over the coastal waters early this\nmorning.\n\nOnshore flow ahead of a low pressure system approaching from the\nnorthwest will help to spread the low clouds into the coastal\nareas tonight. Initially, the clouds will be very low and there is\na chance of locally dense fog on elevated coastal terrain. A \ndeveloping coastal eddy, cyclonic flow aloft and falling heights \nwill cause the marine layer to deepen and the clouds to lift \novernight into Tuesday morning.\n\nWe can expect a general cooling trend through Thursday as the low\npressure system moves into SoCal. Thursday will likely be the\ncoolest day, with temperatures from a few degrees to as much a 15\ndegrees below normal. Ahead of the system, moisture from the low\nlatitudes will likely be drawn into socal with the flow aloft. The\nsystem will use this moisture to produce light precipitation as\nearly as Wednesday morning. Precipitation will become more\nwidespread, progressing from northwest to southeast across the\nregion and increasing in intensity on Thursday as the system moves\nthrough Socal. Conditions become most unstable on Thursday as\ncolder air moves in aloft from the northwest bringing a chance for\nthunderstorms for Thursday morning through Thursday afternoon.\nPrecipitation will taper off by Friday morning as the upper level\ntrough axis moves east and conditions become more stable.\n\nFor Orange and southwestern San Bernardino Counties, rainfall \ncould range from around 1 inch for the Orange County coastal plain\nto around 1.5 inches on the coastal slopes of the mountains \nwith amounts decreasing from north to south into San Diego \nCounty. For the 24 hr period ending at 5 pm Thursday, there is a\n35 to 65 percent chance of greater than 1 inch of rainfall in San\nDiego County from the coast to the mountains. There is about a 30\nto 45 percent chance of 1 inch or more in the Inland Empire and\nabout a 45 to 65 percent chance in Orange County, the Santa Ana\nMountains and on the slopes of the San Bernardino Mountains.\n\nThe snow level will drop to around 8,000 ft on Thursday afternoon\nand the highest peaks of the San Bernardino and San Jacinto \nmountains could get small amounts of snow.\n\nThe low pressure system will also bring periods of gusty southwest\nto west winds in the mountains and deserts for Tuesday through\nThursday. Winds will likely be strongest on Thursday and gusts of\nup to 40 mph could occur in the wind-prone locations.\n\nDry and warmer weather will return for the weekend under high\npressure aloft, with temperatures just below normal inland and \njust above normal in the coastal areas.",
